---
/**
 * The landing page's body.
 *
 * THIS WAS VARIANT A, and it won (Oleh, 2026-08-20). Three candidates shipped behind a switch on
 * 2026-08-19 so the choice could be made by reading rather than by describing: A path over product
 * screenshots with the documentation as tiles, B the same without the path, C the same with the
 * documentation as a list. The switch, the two losing components and `lib/landing-switch.ts` are
 * deleted in the same commit that inlines this one, which is what they were built for.
 *
 * WHY IT WON, as far as the evidence goes: it is the only one of the three that answered all three
 * complaints at once. It onboards (the path), it opens the roadmap (the strip), and it is not a wall
 * of links (tiles and screenshots). Its cost is height, and the measured page is 2463px against
 * B's 1978px.
 *
 * THE ORDER IS THE READER'S SEQUENCE. Which platform are you on, how do you set one up, where is
 * everything, what is coming, and how do you reach a person. The chooser leads because the landing
 * is the only screen where asking who you are is free; everywhere else the same question is a chip
 * in the sidebar that starts with everything on.
 *
 * A DRAWS CARDS, SO IT CAN ADMIT AN EMPTY ROOM. `SHOW_DRAFT_FLAGS` is that switch: a card promises
 * more than a link, and the reader is often here because a backup just failed. It is off while the
 * documentation is still filling in, and it is one line to turn on.
 *
 * THE PER-CARD GLYPH is keyed by href and falls back to the band's own, so a doc link that moves
 * loses its portrait and never loses its card.
 *
 * THE STEP STRIP NOW REACTS TO THE CHOOSER ABOVE IT, and the client's own words are the reason:
 * "we have some selector, and it just picks where to start the integration". Naming your platform
 * used to write the site-wide preference and navigate away, while every word on this page carried
 * on as if nobody had answered. Step 1 now points at that platform's connecting page and each
 * sentence takes that platform's noun, from the one `vocab` in `lib/platforms.ts`.
 *
 * NOTHING IS HIDDEN BY A CHOICE. This page is re-labelled and ADDED TO, never filtered: no section,
 * link or count is ever taken away from a reader who named a platform. Hiding is what the sidebar
 * chips do on a documentation page, where the reader is mid-task and already knows what they want.
 * On the one screen a first visitor sees, subtracting is the wrong instrument.
 *
 * ── ONE LANDING. THE `filter` VARIANT IS DELETED (Oleh, 2026-08-21) ─────────────────────────
 * From 2026-08-19 two treatments shipped together behind a floating switch, differing by exactly
 * one thing: what a click on a platform card did. `reactive` — the card is a DOOR, it narrows the
 * shared preference and takes the reader into that platform's documentation. `filter` — the card
 * was a SELECTOR, the page stayed, and the strip below re-read the preference in place.
 *
 * Dan, on the live portal: V2 will not work at three-plus platforms, and the first option with
 * quick access to a platform's documentation is the one to keep. `reactive` wins, and the switch,
 * `lib/shot-switch.ts`, the `?shots=` parameter and the losing markup went in one commit — which is
 * what a comparison instrument is built for.
 *
 * THE MEASURED REASON `filter` LOSES, and it is not a matter of taste: it was a SINGLE-SELECT
 * CONTROL WRITING A MULTI-SELECT PREFERENCE. Every other platform control in this portal — the docs
 * sidebar, the search modal, the chat, and the roadmap since 2026-08-21 — is a checkbox list that
 * can say "Airtable and Notion". The filter cards could say exactly one, so at three-plus platforms
 * the home page would state LESS than every other surface, and a reader who had ticked two
 * elsewhere would meet, on the front page, a control that could not represent their own choice.
 *
 * WHAT REPLACES ITS ONE GOOD IDEA — a control that scopes without navigating — is the shared
 * `PlatformPicker` in the hero (`components/SupportHero.astro`), which is that same multi-select
 * every other surface draws. So the page now carries two DIFFERENT ACTS rather than two versions
 * of one: the picker says "these are the platforms I care about" and scopes; a card says "take me
 * there now" and narrows on the way out because it is a door.
 *
 * The pre-paint inline script below survives the deletion, minus its variant half: it still stamps
 * the named platform on `<html>` before this markup is parsed, so the re-labelling below is never
 * seen flipping. The attribute is `data-bo-platform` — it was `data-bo-platform`, named after a
 * switch that no longer exists, and the platform reactivity was never part of the variant.
 */import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { BANDS, PLATFORM_TOPICS, ROADMAP_TILES, WAYS_OUT } from '../../lib/landing';
import { STEPS, GLOSSARY_ROWS, bodyParts, nounReadings } from '../../lib/landing-steps';
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
import PlatformMark from '../PlatformMark.astro';
/* DOCUMENTED SUBSET, NOT THE IDENTITY CATALOGUE — see `lib/documented-platforms.ts`. A chip for a
   platform with no pages is a filter that hides everything and offers nothing back. */
import { DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS } from '../../lib/documented-platforms';
import RequestCard from '../RequestCard.astro';
import PlatformStart from '../PlatformStart.astro';
import type { IconName } from '../../lib/icons';

/** See the header: on while A is a candidate would make it lose on the content, not the layout. */
const SHOW_DRAFT_FLAGS = false;

const CARD_ICON: Record<string, IconName> = {
  '/start/getting-started/': 'play',
  '/start/what-baseout-is/': 'shield-check',
  '/start/how-baseout-is-organized/': 'list-checks',
  '/start/signing-in/': 'log-in',

  '/backups/how-backups-work/': 'database-backup',
  '/backups/schedule-and-scope/': 'calendar',
  '/backups/running-a-backup/': 'play',
  '/backups/reading-a-run/': 'history',
  '/backups/retention-and-cleanup/': 'clock',

  '/restore/restoring-a-base/': 'rotate-ccw',
  '/restore/attachments/': 'download',

  '/connections/sources/': 'plug',
  '/connections/destinations/': 'globe',
  '/connections/reconnecting/': 'lock',

  '/schema/': 'table-2',
  '/data/records/': 'search',
  '/data/attachments/': 'download',
  '/data/presets-and-export/': 'square-arrow-out-up-right',

  '/troubleshooting/backup-failed/': 'triangle-alert',
  '/troubleshooting/connection-needs-reconnecting/': 'lock',
  '/troubleshooting/missing-bases/': 'circle-slash',
  '/troubleshooting/what-baseout-cannot-capture/': 'circle-dashed',
};

/**
 * THE PER-PLATFORM HALF OF THE DIRECTORY, resolved against the real content collection.
 *
 * THE LABEL IS THE PAGE'S OWN `title`, read at build time rather than typed here. A link's text is
 * the one thing it cannot afford to be wrong about, and the titles genuinely differ per platform in
 * ways a template would get wrong: Airtable has "field types", Notion has "property types", and
 * ClickUp splits its columns in two and says so in the title.
 *
 * A TOPIC WITH NO PAGE IS SKIPPED rather than rendered dark, so this block cannot mint a dead
 * internal link — which is precisely the defect this app shipped last (`/chat` in the sidebar,
 * 404 on all 41 pages, every other gate green).
 */
const docTitles = new Map<string, string>();
for (const entry of await getCollection('docs')) docTitles.set(entry.id, entry.data.title);

function platformTopics(bandKey: string, id: string) {
  return PLATFORM_TOPICS.filter((t) => t.band === bandKey)
    .map((t) => ({ ...t, title: docTitles.get(`platforms/${id}/${t.slug}`) }))
    .filter((t): t is typeof t & { title: string } => Boolean(t.title));
}

/* THE ROADMAP CARD IS GONE FROM "STILL STUCK?" (Oleh, 2026-08-19), in all three variants, because
   all three carry the roadmap strip: the board is already opened twice above, by three real request
   rows and by the link beside their heading. A third route to the same page, dressed as one of the
   things to do when the documentation failed you, is the page arguing with itself about what the
   reader should do next. */
const EXITS = WAYS_OUT.filter((w) => w.href !== '/roadmap/');

/* AIRTABLE IS ABSENT FROM THIS LIST ON PURPOSE. It is the platform the screenshots ARE, so its
   frame is the screenshot and it needs no substitute. The other two get their own vocabulary in the
   same frame, because there is no ClickUp or Notion product screen and there will not be one: the
   features do not exist yet, and a fabricated screenshot of a feature that does not exist is the
   one mistake a support portal cannot recover from. */
/* Airtable is the reader's assumed default, so the glossary teaches the OTHER vocabularies — and
   only the ones with pages behind them. Derived, because `PLATFORMS` now carries identities the
   product does not support: at five it would teach a reader two vocabularies they can never use. */
const GLOSSARY_PLATFORMS = DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS.filter((p) => p.id !== 'airtable');


/* NO COUNT IN THE SUBHEAD. It said "22 pages", which is the number of links this block draws and
   not the number of pages the documentation has (38, and the sidebar shows every one of them). A
   reader who opens the sidebar catches the discrepancy in a second, and a support portal that is
   caught being loose with a number it did not have to print has spent something it needs. */
---

{/* `not-content` is load-bearing, not tidiness. This renders inside `.sl-markdown-content`, whose
    prose rhythm is a GENERAL-SIBLING rule: it puts `margin-top` on every second-and-later child of
    every nested flex or grid box in here. Measured before it was added: steps 02 and 03 carried
    `margin-top: 4px` and step 01 did not, so the row of three sat 4px out of true and the cause was
    nowhere in this file. It is the same rule that made the roadmap board look crooked in August. */}
{/* THE PRE-PAINT STAMP, and it is `is:inline` for the one reason that justifies an unchecked
    script: a module script is deferred, so it runs AFTER this markup has painted, and the platform
    re-labelling would be visible flipping. Stamped here, before the strip below is even parsed, the
    browser has the answer at first paint and the stylesheet does the rest. `lib/landing-strip.ts`
    is the authority a moment later and will correct anything this cheap read got wrong; the storage
    key is repeated verbatim because an inline script cannot import it.

    ITS VARIANT HALF IS GONE with the `filter` treatment (2026-08-21). What is left is the half that
    was never about the variant: which platform the reader has named.

    Two platforms selected leaves the stamp OFF, which is the neutral copy: "which Bases or Spaces
    to back up" is not a sentence, and a reader with two platforms on has not told us whose nouns
    are theirs. */}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var r = document.documentElement;
    /* The three that have documentation, repeated verbatim because an inline script cannot import
       `DOCUMENTED_PLATFORM_IDS`. Same admitted duplication as the storage key below, same absence
       of a gate that would catch it drifting. */
    var DOC = ['airtable', 'clickup', 'notion'];
    try {
      var q = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);

      var raw = window.localStorage.getItem('bo-platforms');
      var stored = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
      var fromUrl = q.get('platform');
      var chosen = fromUrl
        ? fromUrl.split(',')
        : Array.isArray(stored)
          ? stored
          : [];

      /* THE ONE-PLATFORM STAMP, unchanged: the steps take a platform's nouns only when the reader
         has named exactly one, because "which Bases or Spaces to back up" is not a sentence. */
      var only = chosen.length === 1 ? chosen[0] : null;
      if (DOC.indexOf(only) !== -1) r.dataset.boPlatform = only;

      /* THE STRIP'S NARROWING. `?cards=` is the layout-review parameter and it OWNS the strip while
         it is present — presence, not value, so this and `lib/landing-strip.ts` cannot disagree.
         Nothing chosen, everything chosen, or a choice that names no documented platform all leave
         the block drawing every card: an empty selection means no filter (the picker's own rule),
         and a block that empties itself is the one failure this must not have. */
      if (!q.has('cards')) {
        var shown = DOC.filter(function (id) {
          return chosen.indexOf(id) !== -1;
        });
        if (shown.length && shown.length < DOC.length) {
          r.dataset.boPlatforms = shown.join(' ');
          r.style.setProperty('--ps-shown', String(shown.length));
        }
      }
    } catch (e) {
      /* No stamp is the neutral reading: every noun ordinary, every card drawn. */
    }
  })();
</script>

<div class="vb ja not-content">
  <!-- 1 · WHO ARE YOU -->

  <PlatformStart />

  <!-- 2 · THE PATH -->

  <section class="vb-sec vb-sec-lead" aria-labelledby="vb-path-h">
    <div class="vb-head">
      <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-path-h">Set up a backup</h2>
      <p class="vb-sub">Three steps, once per Space. Each one opens the page that covers it.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="vb-steps">
      {
        STEPS.map((s) => {
          const parts = bodyParts(s);
          return (
            <li class="vb-step">
              <span class="vb-step-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(s.n).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <h3 class="vb-step-title">
                <a class="vb-step-link" href={s.href} data-step-link={String(s.n)}>
                  {s.title}
                  <span class="vb-arrow"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} /></span>
                </a>
              </h3>
              <p class="vb-step-body">{parts.before}{nounReadings(s).map((r) => (
                <span class="vb-noun" data-for={r.key}>{r.text}</span>
              ))}{parts.after}</p>
              <span class="vb-shot">
                <img
                  class="vb-shot-light"
                  src={`/screens/${s.shot}-light.png`}
                  alt={s.alt}
                  width="1040"
                  height="650"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
                <img
                  class="vb-shot-dark"
                  src={`/screens/${s.shot}-dark.png`}
                  alt={s.alt}
                  width="1040"
                  height="650"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
                {GLOSSARY_PLATFORMS.map((p) => (
                  <span class="vb-glo" data-plat={p.id}>
                    <span class="vb-glo-head">
                      <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={16} />
                      <span class="vb-glo-name">{p.name}</span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="vb-glo-rows">
                      {GLOSSARY_ROWS.map((row) => (
                        <span
                          class="vb-glo-row"
                          data-focus={s.glossaryFocus.includes(row.from) ? '' : undefined}
                        >
                          <span class="vb-glo-k">{row.label}</span>
                          <span class="vb-glo-v">{p.vocab[row.from]}</span>
                        </span>
                      ))}
                    </span>
                  </span>
                ))}
              </span>
            </li>
          );
        })
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 · THE DIRECTORY -->

  <section class="vb-sec" aria-labelledby="vb-dir-h">
    <div class="vb-head">
      <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-dir-h">The documentation</h2>
      <p class="vb-sub">Grouped by what you are doing at the time.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ja-dir">
      {
        BANDS.map((band) => (
          <section class="ja-band">
            <div class="ja-head">
              <h3 class="ja-verb">
                <Icon name={band.icon} size={14} />
                {band.verb}
              </h3>
              <p class="ja-line">{band.line}</p>
            </div>

            <ul class="ja-grid">
              {band.items.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a class="ja-card" href={item.href}>
                    <span class="ja-ico">
                      <Icon name={CARD_ICON[item.href] ?? band.icon} size={18} />
                    </span>
                    <span class="ja-body">
                      <span class="ja-label">
                        {item.label}
                        {SHOW_DRAFT_FLAGS && !item.written && (
                          <span class="ja-flag">Not written yet</span>
                        )}
                      </span>
                      <span class="ja-blurb">{item.blurb}</span>
                    </span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>

            {DOCUMENTED_PLATFORMS.map((p) => {
              const topics = platformTopics(band.key, p.id);
              return topics.length ? (
                <div class="ja-plat" data-plat={p.id}>
                  <h4 class="ja-plat-h">
                    <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={14} />
                    Specific to {p.name}
                  </h4>
                  <ul class="ja-grid">
                    {topics.map((t) => (
                      <li>
                        <a class="ja-card" href={`/platforms/${p.id}/${t.slug}/`}>
                          <span class="ja-ico">
                            <Icon name={t.icon} size={18} />
                          </span>
                          <span class="ja-body">
                            <span class="ja-label">{t.title}</span>
                            <span class="ja-blurb">{t.blurb}</span>
                          </span>
                        </a>
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              ) : null;
            })}
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 · THE ROADMAP -->

  <section class="vb-sec" aria-labelledby="vb-road-h">
    <div class="vb-head vb-head-row">
      <div>
        <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-road-h">From the roadmap</h2>
        <p class="vb-sub">One in progress, one shipped, one planned.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="vb-more" href="/roadmap/">
        See the whole roadmap
        <span class="vb-arrow"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} /></span>
      </a>
    </div>

    {/* THE BOARD'S OWN CARD, IMPORTED RATHER THAN IMITATED. This strip used to draw its own tile —
        a status badge on top, a title, a summary and a dated line with a calendar glyph — which was
        the fifth copy of a card that had already been written four times and drifted, and the
        extraction of `RequestCard.astro` exists to end exactly that. A landing that shows the
        roadmap in a different shape from the roadmap teaches the reader two cards for one thing.

        AND THE VOTE BUTTON IS WIRED, which is the one real objection to reusing it. `lib/board.ts`
        runs on `/roadmap/` and on every request detail page, and its voting half is deliberately
        UNCONDITIONAL — a bail-out on the missing board once took the vote down on nine detail pages
        and looked wired the whole time, which is why it is written that way. Calling `wireBoard()`
        from this page's script therefore costs nothing but the vote, and a dead control on a
        landing page would have been worse than no control at all. */}
    <ul class="vb-road">
      {ROADMAP_TILES.map(({ item }) => (
        <li>
          <RequestCard r={item} teaser />
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 · THE WAYS OUT -->

  <section class="vb-sec" aria-labelledby="vb-out-h">
    <div class="vb-head">
      <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-out-h">Still stuck?</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="vb-ways">
      {
        EXITS.map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a class="vb-way" href={w.href} data-open-chat={w.opensChat ? '' : undefined}>
              <span class="vb-way-i">
                <Icon name={w.icon} size={16} />
              </span>
              <span class="vb-way-t">{w.title}</span>
              <span class="vb-way-b">{w.body}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

<script>
  import { mountLandingStrip } from '../../lib/landing-strip';
  import { wireBoard } from '../../lib/board';
  import { OPEN_CHAT_EVENT } from '../../lib/rows';

  /* The vote control on the three roadmap cards. `wireBoard` does two independent jobs and only the
     filtering half looks for `[data-board]`; the voting half is unconditional by design, so this is
     the supported way to have a working card outside `/roadmap/`. Without it the card would render
     a button that silently does nothing, which is the one honest argument against reusing it. */
  wireBoard();

  /* The strip's reaction to the platform the reader named. It sets one attribute on `<html>`; the
     stylesheet below does the rest. */
  mountLandingStrip();

  /* Scoped to this body rather than the whole document: the chat card is not the only thing on the
     portal that opens the drawer, and a document-wide selector here would fight the others. The
     `[data-landing-body="a"]` wrapper this used to hang off went with the preview switch. */
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll('.ja [data-open-chat]')) {
    el.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(OPEN_CHAT_EVENT, { detail: {} }));
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* ── The frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * No inline padding: the Starlight container already supplies it, and adding a second one is how
   * the reading column ends up narrower here than on every other page. `min-width: 0` appears on
   * every grid child below for the one reason it always does: a grid item's default `auto` minimum
   * is its content, and one unbreakable string then pushes the whole page sideways at 390px.
   */
  /* THE ROOT CARRIES BOTH CLASSES ON PURPOSE. This file was made by transplanting sections out of
     another one, and the transplant renamed the root to `ja` while leaving the frame rule keyed to
     `.vb` — so for several rounds A and B rendered with no width cap and no block padding while C
     had both, and the "one variable at a time" comparison was quietly carrying two. Scoped styles
     make that silent: the selector is valid, it simply matches nothing. */
  .vb {
    max-width: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-block: 32px 64px;
  }

  .vb-sec + .vb-sec {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }

  /* THE ONE SECTION GAP THE SIBLING RULE ABOVE CANNOT REACH, and the client felt it before anyone
     measured it: "Set up a backup" sticks very hard to the three platform cards above it. The
     reason is structural. `PlatformStart` renders its own `<section class="ps">`, so the first
     `.vb-sec` on this page has no `.vb-sec` before it and `+` never fires — measured at 0px between
     the two section boxes, and 8px from the bottom of the card grid to the heading, against 56px
     everywhere else on the page. Named explicitly rather than reached with `:first-of-type`, which
     would match nothing here: `.ps` is also a `section`, so the path is the SECOND one. */
  .vb-sec-lead {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }

  .vb-head {
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .vb-head-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .vb-h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-20);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .vb-sub {
    margin: 4px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* An arrow that inherits its label's colour. `line-height: 0` on the wrapper is not cosmetic:
     an inline box carrying an svg otherwise contributes its own line box and inflates the line it
     sits in, which is exactly the bug that shipped on the header once already. */
  .vb-arrow {
    display: inline-block;
    line-height: 0;
    margin-left: 4px;
    vertical-align: -0.15em;
  }

  .vb-more {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-4);
    transition: opacity 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-more:hover {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .vb-more:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  /* ── 1 · The path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * THE THREE COLUMNS MUST BE THE SAME WIDTH, and the first version was not: asymmetric cell
   * padding (`0 24px` on the ends, `24px` both sides plus a border in the middle) left the middle
   * content box 25px narrower than the first, so the middle SHOT rendered 16px shorter and the row
   * lost the shared baseline it was built for. Measured at 1440: 360 · 334 · 358.
   *
   * So the breathing room is a `column-gap` and the dividers are pseudo-elements sitting in it,
   * which take no width from the track. The unbroken rule across the top was never at risk from a
   * gap: `border-top` is on the LIST, not on the cells, so it is one line whatever the columns do.
   */
  .vb-steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: 40px;
    row-gap: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* `position: relative` is here so the title link can stretch over the whole column, screenshot
     included: one link in the accessibility tree, one Tab stop, a target the size of the step. */
  .vb-step {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 16px 0 0;
  }

  /* The divider, drawn in the middle of the gap so no track pays for it. It starts below the top
     rule rather than at it, so the two hairlines meet as a T rather than crossing. */
  .vb-step + .vb-step::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 16px;
    bottom: 0;
    left: -20px;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* 11px is the floor, and only ever here: an uppercase tracked micro-label, never prose. */
  .vb-step-n {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--bo-ornament);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .vb-step-title {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .vb-step-link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-step-link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .vb-step-body {
    margin: 8px 0 20px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* `margin-top: auto` is what puts three shots on one baseline without a fixed body height: the
     column is a flex column, so the last child absorbs whatever the sentence above did not use. */
  .vb-shot {
    display: block;
    position: relative;
    margin-top: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    transition: border-color 200ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-shot img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: top left;
  }

  .vb-step:hover .vb-shot,
  .vb-step:focus-within .vb-shot {
    border-color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .vb-step:hover .vb-step-link,
  .vb-step:focus-within .vb-step-link {
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .vb-step-link:focus-visible::after {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
  }

  /* ── The theme swap ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * A raster cannot follow a theme, so both exist and one is hidden. Three states have to be right,
   * not two: Starlight stamps `data-theme="light" | "dark"` on the root when the reader chooses,
   * and stamps NOTHING on the default setting, where only `prefers-color-scheme` separates them.
   * Hence the media query is guarded with `:not([data-theme='light'])` rather than left bare: an OS
   * in dark mode plus an explicit light choice is a real combination, and a bare media query hands
   * that reader the dark screenshot on a white page.
   *
   * `display`, not `content-visibility`: the latter does not remove the box, it defers its
   * rendering, so both shots would still occupy the frame.
   */
  .vb-shot-light {
    display: block;
  }

  .vb-shot-dark {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme='light']) .vb-shot-light {
      display: none;
    }

    :root:not([data-theme='light']) .vb-shot-dark {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  :root[data-theme='dark'] .vb-shot-light {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-theme='dark'] .vb-shot-dark {
    display: block;
  }

  :root[data-theme='light'] .vb-shot-light {
    display: block;
  }

  :root[data-theme='light'] .vb-shot-dark {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── The noun that changes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * EVERY READING OF EVERY SENTENCE IS SERVER-RENDERED and one of them is shown. The alternative
   * was rewriting `textContent` from a script, and it loses twice: the neutral sentence would be
   * painted first and visibly swapped a moment later, and the swap would depend on a module that a
   * blocked script kills. Here the choice is one attribute on `<html>`, and a reader who has named
   * their platform never sees anybody else's noun.
   *
   * `display: none` on the readings that lost is not a filter and does not remove information: they
   * are four spellings of ONE sentence, and a hidden one is neither read out nor copied.
   */
  .vb-noun {
    display: none;
  }

  .vb-noun[data-for='neutral'] {
    display: inline;
  }

  :root[data-bo-platform] .vb-noun[data-for='neutral'] {
    display: none;
  }

  :root[data-bo-platform='airtable'] .vb-noun[data-for='airtable'],
  :root[data-bo-platform='clickup'] .vb-noun[data-for='clickup'],
  :root[data-bo-platform='notion'] .vb-noun[data-for='notion'] {
    display: inline;
  }

  /* ── The frame's other occupant, for a platform we have no screen of ────────────────────────
   * IT MUST NOT CHANGE THE FRAME'S HEIGHT, or the strip jumps when the platform changes and the
   * whole page below it moves. The guarantee is structural rather than measured: `.vb-shot` owns
   * an `aspect-ratio` and this card is `position: absolute; inset: 0`, so it contributes nothing
   * to the box no matter what it holds. Measured anyway, at the 72rem cap: the three tracks are
   * (1152 - 80) / 3 = 357.33px, so the frame is 223.33px tall with a screenshot in it and 223.33px
   * tall with this in it.
   *
   * IT READS AS A CARD, NOT AS A MISSING IMAGE. A brand mark, a name, and four rows of that
   * platform's own words: the same information the screenshot would have carried, stated rather
   * than photographed. What it must never look like is a placeholder, because a placeholder tells
   * the reader the page is unfinished when in fact it is being honest.
   *
   * THE INNER CAP IS FOR THE STACKED READING. Below 60rem the strip is one column, so the frame is
   * as wide as the page; without it the four rows would stretch across 900px with a word at each
   * end. 20rem is the width of the widest of the three cards at the three-across reading, so the
   * card looks the same at both.
   */
  .vb-glo {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 16px;
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
  }

  :root[data-bo-platform='clickup'] .vb-glo[data-plat='clickup'],
  :root[data-bo-platform='notion'] .vb-glo[data-plat='notion'] {
    display: flex;
  }

  /* The screenshot steps aside for it. Higher specificity than the theme-swap rules ABOVE, on
     purpose and not by accident: those decide WHICH raster a theme gets, this decides whether a
     raster belongs in this frame at all, and that question is asked first. (0,3,1) against (0,3,0),
     so it would still win if the two blocks were ever reordered. */
  :root[data-bo-platform='clickup'] .vb-shot > img,
  :root[data-bo-platform='notion'] .vb-shot > img {
    display: none;
  }

  .vb-glo-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    max-width: 20rem;
    padding-bottom: 12px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .vb-glo-name {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .vb-glo-rows {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    max-width: 20rem;
  }

  .vb-glo-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  /* 11px, and this is the sanctioned reading of that floor: an uppercase tracked micro-label, never
     prose. The word beside it is the content and takes the ordinary 13px. */
  .vb-glo-k {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .vb-glo-v {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-align: right;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  /* THE ROWS THIS STEP IS NOT ABOUT STEP BACK, so three cards in a row read as three steps rather
     than as one template drawn three times. It is a colour change and nothing else: no size, no
     weight on the label, no box — so no row moves and the card's height is untouched whichever
     rows are marked. `--bo-muted` is the documented floor for text a person reads (5.92 on a white
     card); the dimmer `--bo-ornament` is for separators, and every word here is information. */
  .vb-glo-row:not([data-focus]) .vb-glo-v {
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── 2 · The directory, as tiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * THE ONE THING THAT DIFFERS FROM C. A bordered tile with a glyph gives the eye a target that is
   * not a word, which is the answer to "the page is a wall of links". What it costs is height and a
   * promise: a card says there is something here, and sixteen of these open a page that says "not
   * written yet". C's list pays neither cost and offers neither target, and that trade is the
   * question the pair exists to settle. B renders this block identically and differs by dropping
   * the path above it.
   */
  .ja-dir {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding-top: 16px;
  }

  /* RHYTHM IS TWO GAPS, NOT ONE. Head to grid is 12px and band to band is 64px across a hairline.
     One repeated gap is what makes a long index read as a wall; the pulse is what lets someone
     scroll past four bands and still know where a band starts. */
  .ja-band + .ja-band {
    margin-top: 32px;
    padding-top: 32px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  .ja-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  /* A LABEL, not a control: filled, unbordered, and it never takes a hover. */
  .ja-verb {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex: none;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 4px 8px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-4);
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ja-line {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }
  .ja-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 12px;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* The `li` stretches so cards in a row share a height and the glyph column stays a column. */
  .ja-grid > li {
    display: flex;
  }
  .ja-card {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      border-color 150ms ease-out,
      background-color 150ms ease-out;
  }
  .ja-card:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-primary);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
  }
  .ja-card:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }
  /* 20px square = the label's line box, so the glyph centres on the first line of text without a
     nudge, and every card's glyph therefore sits on the same baseline as every other card's. */
  .ja-ico {
    flex: none;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
    transition: color 150ms ease-out;
  }
  .ja-card:hover .ja-ico {
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }
  .ja-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .ja-label {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }
  /* Words, muted, no hue: an unwritten page is incomplete, not broken. */
  .ja-flag {
    margin-left: 8px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ja-blurb {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── The half of the directory that belongs to one platform ──────────────────────────────────
   * THIS IS WHAT THE `filter` VARIANT EXISTS TO SHOW. Ten of the site's pages exist once per
   * platform, and until now the landing did not list any of them: they sat three levels down a
   * collapsed sidebar group, so the chooser at the top of the page narrowed a documentation set the
   * reader could not see. Naming a platform now brings its own pages up into the band they belong
   * to, which is the client's ask in his words — "the content and the documentation below adapt to
   * the chosen topic".
   *
   * ALL THREE ARE SERVER-RENDERED AND ONE IS SHOWN, the same mechanism as the step nouns above: no
   * reflow of text the browser has to measure, no flash of somebody else's pages, and nothing that
   * stops working when a script is blocked and the reader arrives on `?platform=notion`.
   *
   * IT IS A SUB-BLOCK, NOT MORE CARDS IN THE SAME GRID. Appended to the neutral grid these ten
   * would read as ten more pages that were always there, and the one fact they all share — that
   * they are about ONE platform and appeared because you said so — would be carried by nothing.
   * The heading states it in words; the mark states it before the words are read.
   *
   * IT NO LONGER ASKS WHICH VARIANT IS ON (Oleh, 2026-08-21). It used to require
   * `data-shots='filter'`, which tied the one genuinely useful idea in that variant to the variant
   * itself. The directory does not care HOW the reader chose a platform, only that they have — the
   * hero picker, a card on its way out, or a preference carried in from last week all reach it the
   * same way. That un-gating is what let the `filter` treatment be deleted whole without losing
   * anything: this block is the part of it that was worth keeping, and it had already moved out.
   *
   * `data-bo-platform` ON `:root` makes this (0,2,0) against `.ja-plat`'s (0,1,0), so it still
   * wins regardless of order. That attribute is set only when the preference names exactly ONE
   * platform (`lib/landing-strip.ts`), which is why a reader with two on sees the neutral
   * directory rather than an arbitrary half of their choice.
   */
  .ja-plat {
    display: none;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  :root[data-bo-platform='airtable'] .ja-plat[data-plat='airtable'],
  :root[data-bo-platform='clickup'] .ja-plat[data-plat='clickup'],
  :root[data-bo-platform='notion'] .ja-plat[data-plat='notion'] {
    display: block;
  }

  /* 11px, and this is the sanctioned reading of that floor: an uppercase tracked micro-label. It is
     deliberately NOT a second `.ja-verb` chip — a filled chip here would put two labels of equal
     weight in one band and the eye would stop reading it as one section. */
  .ja-plat-h {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .ja-plat > * {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* ── 3 · The roadmap strip ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * THE CARD ITSELF IS `RequestCard.astro` AND NONE OF ITS PAINT IS HERE. An Astro scoped `<style>`
   * does not reach a child component, so a rule written here for `.rb-card` would ship, be valid,
   * match nothing, and leave both gates green — the trap that cost the board its own reset once
   * already. This block owns the TRACK the cards sit in and stops there.
   *
   * `align-items: start` RATHER THAN THE DEFAULT STRETCH. The three summaries are different
   * lengths, so stretching makes the tallest card set the height and the other two grow a band of
   * blank page under a footer rail that has already ended. Aligned to the top, the three cards have
   * one shared y and their own heights, which is exactly how the same card behaves in a board
   * column. Measured: y identical on all three, heights 168 · 188 · 168 at 1440.
   */
  /* STRETCH, NOT START. Three cards side by side that each end where their own summary ran out
     read as three unrelated blocks; squared off they read as one row of the same thing, which is
     what a showcase strip is for (Oleh, 2026-08-21). A board COLUMN keeps `start`, because there
     the cards are stacked and a shared height would mean nothing. */
  .vb-road {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    align-items: stretch;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  /* The `li` is a bare wrapper and stays a BLOCK. `.rb-card` is a block-level grid, so it fills the
     `li` on its own; making the `li` a flex row instead would size the card to its content and need
     a `flex: 1` on the CARD, which this file cannot write — a scoped rule for a child component's
     class matches nothing. `min-width: 0` because a grid item's default `auto` minimum is its
     content, and one unbreakable title would otherwise push the row sideways at 390px. */
  /* `grid` rather than nothing, so the card inside can take `height: 100%` against a definite box.
     The card's own half of this lives in `RequestCard.astro`, because a rule for a child
     component's class written here would carry this file's scope hash and match nothing. */
  .vb-road > li {
    display: grid;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Starlight's prose rhythm is a general-sibling rule that reaches every nested flex or grid box.
     The CARD zeroes its own top margin in its own file, which is where a component's reset has to
     live; this is the `li` that holds it. */
  .vb-road > li > * {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* ── 4 · The ways out ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .vb-ways {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    /* Two, not three: the roadmap card left. `auto-fit` rather than a hard count so the row still
       fills if a third exit ever comes back. */
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .vb-way {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    column-gap: 12px;
    row-gap: 4px;
    align-items: start;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-way:hover {
    border-color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .vb-way:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  .vb-way-i {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    grid-row: span 2;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
    transition: background-color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-way:hover .vb-way-i {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg-hover);
  }

  .vb-way-t {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .vb-way-b {
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* ── Narrow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * EVERY width query lives at the foot of the sheet, and that is a rule rather than a habit: a
   * media query changes WHEN a declaration is considered, never whether it wins, so any later rule
   * at equal specificity silently discards it. Ten of the sixteen dead declarations this repo
   * produced in one week died exactly that way.
   *
   * At 60rem the path stops being a path: three columns side by side is what makes the sequence
   * readable, and stacked it needs the rule to run down the left of each step instead of across
   * the top of the row.
   */
  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .vb-steps {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      border-top: 0;
    }

    .vb-step {
      padding: 16px 0 24px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    }

    /* The vertical divider belongs to the three-across reading only. Stacked, the rule runs across
       the top of each step instead, and a 1px line down the left margin of a phone is the coloured
       side-stripe this system bans by name. */
    .vb-step + .vb-step::before {
      display: none;
    }

    .vb-step:last-child {
      padding-bottom: 0;
    }

    .vb-shot {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .vb-road,
    .vb-ways {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .vb-sec + .vb-sec,
    .vb-sec-lead {
      margin-top: 40px;
    }

    .ja-band + .ja-band {
      margin-top: 24px;
      padding-top: 24px;
    }
  }

  /* Moved here from the middle of the sheet, where they contradicted the rule stated above: a query
     that is not at the foot is one appended rule away from being silently discarded. */
  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .ja-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 38rem) {
    .ja-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    /* The chip and its sentence stop sharing a line before the sentence starts wrapping under a
       chip that is holding a whole column of empty width beside it. */
    .ja-head {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 8px;
    }
  }

  /* ── THE SCREENSHOT STOPS BEING A SCREENSHOT LONG BEFORE A PHONE (2026-08-25) ──────────────────
   * The three step rasters are 1040x650 and the frame is `aspect-ratio: 16 / 10` of whatever column
   * it is in. Measured on the built page:
   *
   *     width      frame     scale     UI text in the raster
   *     1440       357.3     34%       ~4px      three-across
   *     ~960       ~880      85%       ~9px      stacked, one column
   *     800        ~720      69%       ~8px
   *     390        356.0     34%       ~4px      measured on an emulated 390x844
   *
   * At 390 the whole run panel — its column heads, its five base names, its record counts — is
   * drawn about four pixels tall. It is not small, it is not there. PRODUCT.md §3: *"If an element
   * communicates neither state, identity nor affordance, it does not earn its space."* This one is
   * paying 222px of a 844px screen, three times over, plus ~430KB of raster, to communicate nothing.
   * Oleh, 2026-08-25: hide it below 50rem.
   *
   * WHAT IS NOT HIDDEN, and this is the whole reason the rule is written this way. `.vb-shot` is not
   * a picture, it is a FRAME with two occupants: the raster for Airtable, and `.vb-glo` — the
   * platform vocabulary card — for ClickUp and Notion, which is text and is perfectly legible at any
   * width. Hiding the frame would have taken the card with it and cost a ClickUp reader four rows of
   * their own words. So the RASTER goes, the frame stops being a frame, and the card becomes an
   * ordinary block that draws its own edge.
   *
   * With Airtable selected the frame then has no visible child and no aspect ratio, so it collapses
   * to zero and the step is number, title, sentence — measured after: 356x0 at 390.
   *
   * AT THE FOOT OF THE SHEET, per this file's own rule four blocks up, and after every base
   * declaration of `.vb-shot` and `.vb-glo` — `specs/16-responsive.md` §10.1. */
  @media (max-width: 50rem) {
    .vb-shot {
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      border: 0;
      background: none;
      overflow: visible;
    }

    /* THE SELECTOR IS LONG BECAUSE IT HAS TO OUT-RANK THE THEME SWAP, and it was measured twice
       before it did. Astro stamps `[data-astro-cid-…]` onto the last compound of every selector in
       this block, so the real competition is:

         :root[data-theme='dark'] .vb-shot-light[cid]        (0,4,0)   the theme swap
         .vb-shot > img[cid]                                 (0,2,1)   ← first try, computed `block`
         :root .vb-shot > img[cid]                            (0,3,1)   ← second try, also `block`
         :root .vb-step .vb-shot > img[cid]                   (0,4,1)   this one

